   "object type {type-name} holds {objects} objects and this read is capped at {max}. A type whose rows nothing asks for is shipped WHOLE — a `:where` the store cannot answer filters what already arrived, so it cuts nothing off the payload. The way that cuts it: say which rows the app needs, and it binds a QUERY instead of the type — a `:from` variable over this type whose every clause names one of its attributes under `=`, `not=`, `<`, `>`, `<=`, `>=` or a `contains` of a non-blank needle is lowered into the read (sutra.capps.variables/store-where), and only its rows are read, derived and shipped. `truthy`/`falsy` and `in` are NOT lowered — they call a missing value true, which no SQL comparison does. Three ways that avoid the payload instead: an Object View (`.entv`), whose payload is the one-hop neighbourhood of ONE object; a rollup in :derived-properties, which counts over the same edge without delivering rows; or `:aggregate-types`, which is queryable instead of delivered. Raise `settings app-bind-max` to overrule this."
